    "title": "Amy Madigan Wins Oscars For Best Supporting Actress",
    "text": "Amy Madigan is officially an Oscar winner. The veteran actress claimed Best Supporting Actress at the 98th Academy Awards for her haunting performance as Aunt Gladys in Warner Bros.’ horror hit Weapons. The win caps one of the tightest races of the night — a nail-biter that saw the major precursors split three ways between Madigan (Critics Choice and Actor Awards), Golden Globe winner Teyana Taylor (One Battle After Another), and BAFTA champ Wunmi Mosaku (Sinners). The lineup was rounded out by Sentimental Value costars Inga Ibsdotter Lilleaas and Elle Fanning. A horror performance that broke through Madigan’s portrayal of Aunt Gladys — a parasitic witch who survives by feeding off others — became a pop-culture phenomenon, inspiring Halloween costumes, viral memes, and merchandise centered on the film’s unforgettable antagonist. Madigan picked up crucial awards-season wins at the Critics Choice and SAG Actor Awards, giving her strong support from both the media and the acting branch. But those victories didn’t guarantee an Oscar win. Just last year, another horror performance appeared poised for Oscar glory. Demi Moore swept Critics Choice and the SAG Actor Award for The Substance, only to lose BAFTA and ultimately the Best Actress Oscar to Mikey Madison for Anora. A 40-year Oscar journey Madigan was first nominated for an Academy Award in 1986 for Twice in a Lifetime. Her victory tonight comes 40 years later, marking the longest gap between a first nomination and a first Oscar win for an actress. She lost that year to Anjelica Huston for Prizzi’s Honor. Amy Madigan and Ed Harris at 1986 OscarsAMPAS That surpasses the previous record held by Geraldine Page, who waited 32 years between her first nomination for Hondo (1953) and her eventual win for The Trip to Bountiful (1985). Winning without a Best Picture boost Madigan’s triumph is also unusual because Weapons was the film’s only Oscar nomination. Historically, acting winners often benefit from appearing in films with broad Academy support. But only eight women in Oscar history have won Supporting Actress as the sole nominee from their film: Mary Astor — The Great Lie (1941) Claire Trevor — Key Largo (1948) Margaret Rutherford — The V.I.P.s (1963) Goldie Hawn — Cactus Flower (1969) Linda Hunt — The Year of Living Dangerously (1983) Marisa Tomei — My Cousin Vinny (1992) Angelina Jolie — Girl, Interrupted (1999) Penélope Cruz — Vicky Cristina Barcelona (2008) By comparison, her competitors came from films with much broader Academy backing: Sinners led the nominations tally with 16, while One Battle After Another had 13 and Sentimental Value earned nine. A brief but unforgettable performance Madigan’s turn in Weapons lasts only about 14 minutes, placing it among the shortest winning performances in the category’s history. The record remains held by Beatrice Straight (Network) and Judi Dench (Shakespeare in Love), who each won for performances under six minutes. At 75 years old, Madigan also joins the ranks of the Academy’s oldest acting winners, though she falls just short of the Supporting Actress record held by Peggy Ashcroft, who was 77 when she won for A Passage to India. In the end, the Academy embraced Madigan’s unforgettable villain — and delivered a long-awaited Oscar coronation four decades in the making.",
